1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,742
Take three plus two i,
and three minus two i.

2
00:00:03,742 --> 00:00:06,273
Both numbers carry an imaginary part.

3
00:00:06,673 --> 00:00:08,567
Add them, and you get six.

4
00:00:08,567 --> 00:00:10,932
Multiply them, and you get thirteen.

5
00:00:10,932 --> 00:00:12,383
Every i has vanished.

6
00:00:12,833 --> 00:00:16,039
Aarav saw the same idea
on his ride home today.

7
00:00:16,389 --> 00:00:19,947
His friend Ravi waited exactly
opposite, across the road.

8
00:00:19,947 --> 00:00:23,962
Same distance along, just on the
other side of the middle line.

9
00:00:24,362 --> 00:00:26,870
Complex numbers have partners like that.

10
00:00:26,870 --> 00:00:31,214
Today: when are two complex numbers
equal, and what is a conjugate?

11
00:00:31,864 --> 00:00:32,981
First question.

12
00:00:32,981 --> 00:00:35,334
When are two complex numbers equal?

13
00:00:35,684 --> 00:00:40,613
Every complex number has two parts: a
real part, a, and an imaginary part,

14
00:00:40,613 --> 00:00:41,838
b, next to i.

15
00:00:42,238 --> 00:00:46,322
Two complex numbers are equal
only when both parts match.

16
00:00:46,322 --> 00:00:49,369
Real with real, imaginary with imaginary.

17
00:00:49,769 --> 00:00:51,355
Watch the match test.

18
00:00:51,355 --> 00:00:54,488
Three plus two i,
against three plus two i.

19
00:00:54,488 --> 00:00:55,807
Real parts match.

20
00:00:55,807 --> 00:00:57,383
Imaginary parts match.

21
00:00:57,383 --> 00:00:58,283
Equal.

22
00:00:58,411 --> 00:01:01,865
Now three plus two i,
against three minus two i.

23
00:01:01,865 --> 00:01:05,577
The real parts match, but
the imaginary parts do not.

24
00:01:05,577 --> 00:01:06,477
Not equal.

25
00:01:06,873 --> 00:01:10,281
So one complex equation
gives two real equations.

26
00:01:10,281 --> 00:01:12,542
That is the power of equality.

27
00:01:13,192 --> 00:01:14,881
Now, the partner from the hook.

28
00:01:15,231 --> 00:01:18,847
The conjugate of a plus
i b is a minus i b.

29
00:01:19,247 --> 00:01:20,718
Keep the real part.

30
00:01:20,718 --> 00:01:23,240
Flip the sign of the imaginary part.

31
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:24,292
That is all.

32
00:01:24,692 --> 00:01:28,571
We write it as z with a bar
on top, and read it as z bar.

33
00:01:29,321 --> 00:01:30,436
Watch z move.

34
00:01:30,436 --> 00:01:35,576
Its conjugate follows, like a reflection
in the real axis, the middle line.

35
00:01:35,976 --> 00:01:38,707
When z climbs up, z bar dips down.

36
00:01:38,707 --> 00:01:40,423
Real parts stay equal.

37
00:01:40,423 --> 00:01:42,571
Imaginary parts stay opposite.

38
00:01:42,971 --> 00:01:46,247
And when z lands on the
middle line, the two meet.

39
00:01:46,247 --> 00:01:48,506
A real number is its own conjugate.

40
00:01:49,156 --> 00:01:50,056
Quick one.

41
00:01:50,037 --> 00:01:53,379
Is six i its own conjugate,
like a real number?

42
00:01:53,379 --> 00:01:54,625
Pause and think.

43
00:01:57,825 --> 00:01:58,725
No!

44
00:01:58,369 --> 00:02:03,204
Six i is zero plus six i, so
its conjugate is minus six i.

45
00:02:03,204 --> 00:02:06,538
Only real numbers are
their own conjugates.

46
00:02:07,188 --> 00:02:08,350
Side by side.

47
00:02:08,350 --> 00:02:10,502
The conjugate flips one sign.

48
00:02:10,502 --> 00:02:15,881
Three plus two i becomes three minus
two i: a mirror image across the line.

49
00:02:16,281 --> 00:02:18,252
The negative flips both signs.

50
00:02:18,252 --> 00:02:22,657
Three plus two i becomes minus three
minus two i, through the centre.

51
00:02:23,257 --> 00:02:24,357
Now you try.

52
00:02:24,357 --> 00:02:27,552
What is the conjugate
of four minus seven i?

53
00:02:27,552 --> 00:02:28,919
Pause and decide.

54
00:02:32,119 --> 00:02:33,619
Four plus seven i!

55
00:02:33,619 --> 00:02:35,173
Keep the real part.

56
00:02:35,173 --> 00:02:37,331
Flip only the imaginary sign.

57
00:02:37,981 --> 00:02:39,029
Remember this.

58
00:02:39,029 --> 00:02:42,761
Keep the real part, flip the
sign of the imaginary part.

59
00:02:43,461 --> 00:02:45,352
Now the magic from the hook.

60
00:02:45,352 --> 00:02:46,857
Why do the i's vanish?

61
00:02:47,207 --> 00:02:48,133
Step one.

62
00:02:48,133 --> 00:02:49,656
Add z and z bar.

63
00:02:49,656 --> 00:02:52,782
Real parts add, and imaginary parts add.

64
00:02:53,532 --> 00:02:54,432
Step two.

65
00:02:54,421 --> 00:02:56,836
Plus i b and minus i b cancel.

66
00:02:56,836 --> 00:02:59,804
What is left is two a, a real number.

67
00:03:00,554 --> 00:03:01,719
Step three.

68
00:03:01,719 --> 00:03:03,681
Multiply z by z bar.

69
00:03:03,681 --> 00:03:08,782
x plus y, times x minus y,
is x squared minus y squared.

70
00:03:09,532 --> 00:03:10,432
Step four.

71
00:03:10,412 --> 00:03:12,133
i squared is minus one.

72
00:03:12,133 --> 00:03:15,837
So minus i squared b squared
turns into plus b squared.

73
00:03:16,587 --> 00:03:19,857
So z times z bar is a
squared plus b squared.

74
00:03:19,857 --> 00:03:21,917
Always real, never negative.

75
00:03:21,917 --> 00:03:25,373
For three plus two i:
nine plus four, thirteen.

76
00:03:26,023 --> 00:03:27,759
Now, one worked example.

77
00:03:28,109 --> 00:03:33,119
Find the complex number z, if z plus
two times z bar equals nine minus

78
00:03:33,119 --> 00:03:34,019
four i.

79
00:03:34,412 --> 00:03:35,422
Step one.

80
00:03:35,422 --> 00:03:39,140
Let z be x plus i y, with x and y real.

81
00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:41,639
Then z bar is x minus i y.

82
00:03:42,389 --> 00:03:43,387
Step two.

83
00:03:43,387 --> 00:03:49,523
Collect: x plus two x is three x,
and i y minus two i y is minus i y.

84
00:03:50,273 --> 00:03:51,293
Step three.

85
00:03:51,293 --> 00:03:52,768
Equate real parts.

86
00:03:52,768 --> 00:03:55,628
Three x equals nine, so x is three.

87
00:03:56,378 --> 00:03:57,400
Step four.

88
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:59,256
Equate imaginary parts.

89
00:03:59,256 --> 00:04:02,652
Minus y equals minus four, so y is four.

90
00:04:03,402 --> 00:04:05,447
So z is three plus four i.

91
00:04:05,447 --> 00:04:10,637
Check: three plus four i, plus six
minus eight i, is nine minus four i.

92
00:04:11,287 --> 00:04:12,752
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:04:13,102 --> 00:04:18,430
Solve: one plus i, times x, plus one
minus i, times y, equals two plus

94
00:04:18,430 --> 00:04:19,330
four i.

95
00:04:19,205 --> 00:04:22,315
Many students write x is two, y is four.

96
00:04:22,715 --> 00:04:25,401
But x and y sit inside both parts.

97
00:04:25,401 --> 00:04:29,745
Collect first: x plus y is
two, and x minus y is four.

98
00:04:29,745 --> 00:04:32,608
So x is three, and y is minus one.

99
00:04:33,258 --> 00:04:35,043
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:35,043 --> 00:04:39,872
In JEE Main and JEE Advanced,
as equations in z and z bar.

101
00:04:39,872 --> 00:04:43,502
And in PUC board exams,
as direct questions.

102
00:04:44,102 --> 00:04:45,347
Exam level now.

103
00:04:45,347 --> 00:04:49,167
Find the sum of the real
parts of every element of S.

104
00:04:49,167 --> 00:04:50,465
Pause and solve.

105
00:04:55,865 --> 00:04:57,105
The answer is B.

106
00:04:57,105 --> 00:04:58,005
Zero.

107
00:04:58,144 --> 00:05:00,223
Write z as x plus i y.

108
00:05:00,223 --> 00:05:03,853
Real part: x squared
minus y squared plus x.

109
00:05:03,853 --> 00:05:07,178
Imaginary part: y times two x minus one.

110
00:05:07,678 --> 00:05:09,594
Both parts must be zero.

111
00:05:09,594 --> 00:05:12,265
So y is zero, or x is one half.

112
00:05:12,765 --> 00:05:15,622
If y is zero, z is zero or minus one.

113
00:05:15,622 --> 00:05:20,243
If x is one half, y squared is
three quarters: two more solutions.

114
00:05:20,743 --> 00:05:24,391
Real parts: zero, minus
one, one half and one half.

115
00:05:24,391 --> 00:05:25,919
They add up to zero.

116
00:05:26,519 --> 00:05:27,980
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:27,980 --> 00:05:29,481
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:32,681 --> 00:05:35,679
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:05:36,079 --> 00:05:36,979
One.

120
00:05:36,615 --> 00:05:40,032
What is the conjugate of
minus two plus seven i?

121
00:05:40,932 --> 00:05:41,832
Two.

122
00:05:41,553 --> 00:05:46,226
If x plus three i equals
four plus y i, find x and y.

123
00:05:47,126 --> 00:05:48,026
Three.

124
00:05:47,788 --> 00:05:50,532
Why is z times z bar never negative?

125
00:05:51,632 --> 00:05:55,738
Next time: Aarav rides four
kilometres east and three north.

126
00:05:55,738 --> 00:05:57,609
How far, and which way?

127
00:05:57,609 --> 00:05:59,688
The modulus and the argument.
